{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789250150,"nonce":"WeKXfM+BMl8Epny70rfVQTfnsWc=","title":"Addresses were 64-bit integers in HIR; pointer-width types added","message":"`usize`/`isize` converted to `HirType::I64`, a function's address was an\n`I64` value, and the Python frontend and shared library passed\ninstance and code addresses as `i64` to runtime symbols that take\npointers. Right on a 64-bit host, wrong on wasm32, where an i64 argument\ndoes not match an i32 pointer parameter and a null stored as `I32(0)`\nor `I64(0)` has the wrong width for the slot.\n\nLanded: `HirType::USize`/`ISize` and `HirConstant::USize`/`ISize`,\nsized by `zyntax_compiler::target_pointer_size()` (host by default, set\nby a cross-compiling backend); Cranelift and LLVM translate them to the\ntarget word, the interpreter to the host word; `hir_ty_size` and the\nbackends' type sizes use the target pointer size for `Ptr`, `USize`,\n`ISize`; a function address is a `USize` value; a `Null` literal of a\npointer type is `HirConstant::Null`, which Cranelift now materialises\n(it skipped it before, leaving the value undefined). The library and\nthe Python frontend type every address `usize`.\n\nStill 64-bit-only: `lowering.rs` takes `pointer_size` from the host\nrather than `target_pointer_size()`, and the list header's `data` field\nis declared `i64` while stored and loaded as a pointer (the layout is\nthe same on 32-bit because the next field is 8-aligned, but the\ndeclaration is wrong).","files":null}]}